# Records - Field Name Index

**Purpose:** Decisions about how field names resolve on a record — one index, its precedence, and its guards.

## Decision: The name → field Index

**Status:** Adopted

**Context:** With fields addressed by name, every read, write, delete, `has`, and enumeration needs a resolution rule. Scanning section fields or converting labels at access time would make cost and precedence implicit, and duplicate labels in a document would have no defined winner.

**Decision:** Extraction builds one `name → field` index per record, in document order, mapping `labelToName(label)` to the field construct. The first occurrence of a name wins; later duplicates stay in the section, shadowed. Every proxy trap is an index lookup — no access path scans section fields or converts a label. Removing the indexed field — by `delete`, or by a rename that vacates the name — promotes the next duplicate, and the promoted field keeps its own stored label. A label resolving to a reserved name (`name`, `save`, `renameField`) aborts construction with `TypeError: Field label "{label}" resolves to reserved name "{name}"`. Guards come from the name-class table: reserved names throw on read/write/delete, the canonical grammar gates creation only, and not-removed deletes report failure.

**Consequences:** Resolution is an O(1) map lookup per access and precedence is defined. Duplicate labels are tolerated, not repaired: a shadowed field survives in the document until the promoted one is removed. A reserved label is a construction-time failure — a document carrying one cannot be extracted until the label is fixed.
